---
// The home page. Its examples come from the docs and its numbers from the README, at build time,
// so the page says what the repo does at this commit.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeCard from '../components/CodeCard.astro';
import PerfChart from '../components/PerfChart.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { docsIndex } from '../docs-index.mjs';
import { lineHtml } from '../markdown.mjs';
import { errorOf, fences } from '../examples.mjs';
import { buildTime, tickLoop } from '../numbers.mjs';

// parallel.md: its first example, the C that example compiles to (the page's first two C blocks),
// and the example that must fail to compile.
const parallel = fences('reference/parallel.md');
const calx = parallel.filter((f) => f.lang === 'calx');
const c = parallel.filter((f) => f.lang === 'c');
const example = calx[0].code;
const exampleC = `${c[0].code}\n\n// and in main, where the loop was:\n${c[1].code}`;
const failing = calx.map((f) => errorOf(f.code)).find((e) => e !== null);
if (!failing) throw new Error('docs/reference/parallel.md has no example marked // error:');

// What stash reads and answers: the first two text blocks of the guide's first page.
const [reads, answers] = fences('guide/getting-started.md').filter((f) => f.lang === 'text');
const guide = docsIndex().find((s) => s.id === 'guide')!;

const bench = tickLoop();
const windows = bench.platforms[0];
// Times on Windows, the first column.
const check = buildTime('calx check');
const size = check.label.match(/(\d+) modules \(([\d,]+) lines\)/);
if (!size) throw new Error(`README.md's "${check.label}" row doesn't say how many modules and lines`);
const rebuild = buildTime('Debug rebuild').windows;
const cold = buildTime('calx run of a small program').windows;
const unchanged = buildTime('calx run of it again').windows;

// For search engines: the title says what Calx is in the words people search with, and the site's
// name is Calx rather than its domain (schema.org's WebSite, which Google reads for that name).
const site = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'WebSite',
	name: 'Calx',
	alternateName: 'Calx programming language',
	url: new URL('/', Astro.site).href,
};
---

<Base
	title="Calx: a fast, memory-safe, deterministic programming language"
	description="Calx is a general-purpose compiled language: memory-safe without a garbage collector, math defined to the bit, and parallel loops that give the same result."
	jsonLd={site}
>
	<main class="home">
		<section class="hero">
			<canvas class="crystal" data-scene="hero" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
			<div class="hero-copy">
				<div class="hero-text">
					<h1 class="display">Fast, safe code that gives the same answer every time</h1>
					<p class="lede">
						Calx is a general-purpose compiled language. It's memory-safe without a garbage collector, its math is
						defined to the bit on every machine, and its parallel loops give the same result on one core or
						twenty-four.
					</p>
					<div class="cta">
						<a class="btn primary" href="/guide/install/">
							Install Calx <svg class="i" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-arrow"></use></svg>
						</a>
						<a class="btn" href="/guide/getting-started/">Learn Calx</a>
					</div>
					<p class="fineprint">Pre-release: build it from source. Compiles to C, runs on Windows and Linux, MIT licensed.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="split">
				<div>
					<h2 class="h">Parallel loops, checked while the program compiles</h2>
					<p class="intro">
						<code>parallel for</code> runs a loop's iterations across a pool of threads. The checker makes sure no iteration
						touches what another one changes, so the program prints the same thing on one thread or twenty-four.
					</p>
					<ul class="facts">
						<li>The check is the ownership check, applied between iterations. It costs nothing at run time.</li>
						<li>What iterations print comes out in their order, and a panic reports the first iteration that panicked.</li>
						<li>
							Calx compiles to C. The C tab shows what this loop becomes: a function per chunk, its pointers marked
							<code>restrict</code>.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<a class="more" href="/reference/parallel/">Read about parallel loops</a>
				</div>
				<div class="stack">
					<CodeCard calx={example} c={exampleC} file="game.calx" />
					<div class="note">Change something another iteration can see, and it won't compile:</div>
					<pre class="term"><span class="path">main.calx:{failing.line}:{failing.column}:</span> <span class="e">error:</span> <span class="msg">{failing.message}</span>
{'  ' + failing.text}
{'  ' + ' '.repeat(failing.column - 1)}<span class="caret">{'^' + '~'.repeat(failing.token.length - 1)}</span>
<span class="dim">1 error</span></pre>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2 class="h">What Calx promises, and where it's checked</h2>
			<p class="intro">
				Each of these links to the page that defines it, and the examples on those pages are compiled and run on every
				push.
			</p>
			<div class="claims">
				<div class="claim">
					<h3>Faster than Rust, on our benchmark</h3>
					<p>
						The tick-loop benchmark runs the same algorithms in Calx and in Rust, and both must give the same checksum.
						Calx takes about {bench.ratio}× Rust's time, with one thread or 24.
					</p>
					<div class="side">
						<div class="proof"><b>{windows.calx1.toFixed(2)}</b> vs {windows.rust1.toFixed(2)} ms per tick</div>
						<a class="more" href="#perf">The benchmark</a>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<h3>Deterministic math</h3>
					<p>
						Integers wrap, divide and shift, and floats compute and print, the same way on every platform. The rules match
						Java's, so code ported from Java gives the same bits.
					</p>
					<div class="side">
						<div class="proof"><code>-16 &gt;&gt;&gt; 28</code> is <b>15</b>, as in Java</div>
						<a class="more" href="/reference/integers/">Integers and floats</a>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<h3>Memory-safe by default</h3>
					<p>
						Ownership is checked while the program compiles and costs nothing while it runs: no garbage collector, no
						reference counts. Unchecked code is opt-in, inside <code>unsafe</code>.
					</p>
					<div class="side">
						<div class="proof">0 GC pauses, by construction</div>
						<a class="more" href="/reference/ownership/">Ownership</a>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<h3>Strict by default</h3>
					<p>
						No implicit conversions, no dropped values, no unused names and no shadowing. Each mistake is an error that says
						what to write instead.
					</p>
					<div class="side">
						<div class="proof">every rule is an error, not a warning</div>
						<a class="more" href="/reference/strictness/">Strictness</a>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="claim">
					<h3>Fast to build</h3>
					<p>
						<code>calx check</code> reads {size[1]} modules, {size[2]} lines, in {check.windows}. A small program runs in {cold} from
						an empty cache, and in {unchanged} when nothing changed.
					</p>
					<div class="side">
						<div class="proof"><b>{rebuild.replace(/ ms$/, '')}</b> ms to rebuild after one change</div>
						<a class="more" href="/reference/command/">The calx command</a>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section" id="perf">
			<h2 class="h">Measured on every push</h2>
			<p class="intro">
				The tick-loop benchmark is a synthetic simulation, a world of packed block storage, entity physics in doubles, hash
				maps and messages between regions, stepped one tick at a time. We wrote both versions, and CI runs them on every
				push, on Windows and Linux. The raw results are in the repo.
			</p>
			<PerfChart />
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<div class="split learn">
				<div>
					<h2 class="h">Learn it by building one program</h2>
					<p class="intro">
						The guide builds <code>stash</code>, a small inventory program: it reads commands, keeps count of what's put away
						and taken out, and says what went wrong when a command doesn't make sense. Each page adds to it.
					</p>
					<div class="io">
						<div><div class="cap">It reads</div><pre>{reads.code}</pre></div>
						<div><div class="cap">It answers</div><pre>{answers.code}</pre></div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<ol class="steps">
					{
						guide.pages.map((p) => (
							<li>
								<a href={`/${p.slug}/`}>
									<div>
										<b>{p.title}</b>
										<span set:html={lineHtml(p.about, { links: false })} />
									</div>
								</a>
							</li>
						))
					}
				</ol>
			</div>
		</section>

		<div class="band">
			<canvas class="crystal" data-scene="band" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
			<div class="inner">
				<div class="text">
					<h2 class="h">Built with Calx: Calcite</h2>
					<p>
						Calcite is a Minecraft Java Edition server in development, and the largest Calx program so far. Mojang's own
						client joins it in online mode, walks around a flat world and chats, and its login matches the official
						server's packet for packet. Chunk loading and saved worlds are being built now, and world generation, regions on
						every core and plugins come after them.
					</p>
					<p>It aims to behave exactly as the official server does, and to run faster. Neither is measured yet.</p>
					<p class="link"><a class="more" href="https://github.com/ellipog/calcite">Calcite on GitHub</a></p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<SiteFooter />
	</main>
</Base>

<script>
	import '../scripts/crystal.js';
</script>
